response:
The escape speed from a spherical body is

$$v_{e}=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}} $$

so that for the planet

$$v_{e,p}= \sqrt{\frac{2G M_{p}}{R_{p}}}
      =\sqrt{\frac{2G\,k_{M}M_{\oplus}}{k_{R}R_{\oplus}}}
      =\sqrt{\frac{k_{M}}{k_{R}}}\;
        \sqrt{\frac{2GM_{\oplus}}{R_{\oplus}}}
      =\sqrt{\frac{k_{M}}{k_{R}}}\,v_{e,\oplus}$$

with $k_{M}=2.40$ and $k_{R}=1.73$.

For the Earth

$$
v_{e,\oplus}= \sqrt{\frac{2GM_{\oplus}}{R_{\oplus}}}
 =\sqrt{\frac{2(6.674\times10^{-11}\,{\rm N\,m^{2}kg^{-2}})
          (5.972\times10^{24}\,{\rm kg})}{6.371\times10^{6}\,{\rm m}}}
$$

$$
v_{e,\oplus}= \sqrt{\frac{7.967\times10^{14}}{6.371\times10^{6}}}
            =\sqrt{1.25\times10^{8}}\;{\rm m\,s^{-1}}
            \approx 1.12\times10^{4}\ {\rm m\,s^{-1}}
            \approx 11.2\ {\rm km\,s^{-1}} ,
$$

the familiar $11.2\;{\rm km\,s^{-1}}$.

Hence

$$
\frac{v_{e,p}}{v_{e,\oplus}}=\sqrt{\frac{2.40}{1.73}}
      =\sqrt{1.387}\approx 1.18 .
$$

$$
v_{e,p}\approx1.18\,v_{e,\oplus}\approx1.18\times11.2\;{\rm km\,s^{-1}}
      \approx 1.3\!-\!1.4\times10^{1}\ {\rm km\,s^{-1}} .
$$

In absolute numbers

* $M_{p}=2.40M_{\oplus}=2.40\times5.972\times10^{24}\approx1.43\times10^{25}\ {\rm kg}$,
* $R_{p}=1.73R_{\oplus}=1.73\times6371\ {\rm km}\approx1.10\times10^{4}\ {\rm km}
      =1.103\times10^{7}\ {\rm m}$,

$$
v_{e,p}= \sqrt{\frac{2(6.674\times10^{-11})(1.433\times10^{25})}
                    {1.103\times10^{7}}}
       \approx\sqrt{1.73\times10^{8}}\ {\rm m\,s^{-1}}
       \approx1.32\times10^{4}\ {\rm m\,s^{-1}}
$$

i.e. $\approx 13\!-\!13.5\;{\rm km\,s^{-1}}$, about $13.2\;{\rm km\,s^{-1}}$ with the numbers quoted, $\sim 18\%$ larger than the terrestrial escape speed.  Using $v_{e,\oplus}=11.19\;{\rm km\,s^{-1}}$ this is $v_{e,p}\simeq13.2\;{\rm km\,s^{-1}}$, essentially the $ \sim 13\;{\rm km/s}$ often quoted.

For a parallel plate

$$C=\kappa \,\varepsilon_{0}\frac{A}{d}\qquad 
U=\frac12\,C V^{2}$$

is the electrostatic energy on the plates.

* $A=128\;{\rm cm^{2}}=128\times10^{-4}\;{\rm m^{2}}=1.28\times10^{-2}\;{\rm m^{2}}$
* $d=1.87\;{\rm mm}=1.87\times10^{-3}\;{\rm m}$

$$
C=\kappa\varepsilon_{0}\frac{A}{d}
   =7.1\;\frac{8.854\times10^{-12}\;{\rm F/m}\times1.28\times10^{-2}\;{\rm m^{2}}}
          {1.87\times10^{-3}\;{\rm m}}
   \approx 4.3\times10^{-10}\ {\rm F}\approx 430\;{\rm pF}
$$

and on $V=18\;{\rm V}$

$$
U=\frac12CV^{2}
  =\frac12\,(4.30\times10^{-10}{\rm F})(18{\rm V})^{2}
  =\frac12\,(4.30\times10^{-10})(324)\ {\rm J}
  \approx 6.9\!-\!7.0\times10^{-8}\ {\rm J}
$$

$$
U\approx 7\times10^{-8}\ {\rm J}\approx 0.07\;\mu{\rm J}
$$

i.e. about $6\!-\!7\times10^{-8}\,{\rm J}$, $\approx 0.07\,\mu{\rm J}$ on the
$18\,{\rm V}$ supply.  With $\varepsilon_{0}=8.85\times10^{-12}\,$F/m
$C\approx4.30\times10^{-10}\,$F and $U\approx6.97\times10^{-8}\,{\rm J}
\approx0.069\!-\!0.070\,\mu{\rm J}$, essentially $ \sim 0.07\,\mu{\rm J}$ of
electrostatic energy.

For a frictionless bank the normal reaction $N$ supplies the centripetal
force and the weight.  With the bank angle $\theta$ measured from the
horizontal

* $N\cos\theta = mg$  vertical equilibrium
* $N\sin\theta = m v^{2}/r$  horizontal centripetal requirement

Hence

$$
\tan\theta=\frac{N\sin\theta}{N\cos\theta}=\frac{v^{2}/r}{g}
      =\frac{v^{2}}{rg}\;,
$$

the familiar ideal banking condition.

$$
v=70\;{\rm km\,h^{-1}}=\frac{70\times10^{3}}{3600}\;{\rm m\,s^{-1}}
   =19.44\;{\rm m\,s^{-1}}
$$

$$
v^{2}= \left(\frac{70}{3.6}\right)^{2}=3.78\times10^{2}\ {\rm m^{2}s^{-2}}
$$

$$
rg = 397\times 9.81 = 3.895\times10^{3}\ {\rm m^{2}s^{-2}}
$$

$$
\tan\theta =\frac{v^{2}}{rg}
           =\frac{(70/3.6)^{2}}{397\times9.81}
           \approx\frac{3.78\times10^{2}}{3.894\times10^{3}}
           \approx 9.7\times10^{-2}
$$

$$
\theta =\arctan\!\left[\frac{v^{2}}{rg}\right]
       =\arctan 0.0971\approx 9.68\times10^{-2}\ {\rm rad}
$$

$$
\theta\approx0.0968\;{\rm rad}\approx0.0968\times\frac{180}{\pi}
      \approx 5.5^{\circ}
$$

i.e. about $5\!-\!6^{\circ}$ to the horizontal, $\,\tan\theta\approx0.10$,
about $5.5^{\circ}\approx5.54^{\circ}$ with the numbers quoted.  This is
the ideal frictionless banking angle; in practice a small extra angle
is needed to allow for tyre friction.

For a circular Kepler orbit

$$T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^{3}}{\mu}}\qquad 
\mu =GM_{\oplus}$$

is the time for one revolution about the centre of the Earth.

* Geocentric radius

$$
r=R_{\oplus}+h
$$

$$
r = 6371\;{\rm km}+748\;{\rm km}=7119\;{\rm km}
      =7.119\times10^{6}\ {\rm m}
$$

* $r^{3}/\mu$

$$
r^{3}= \left(7.119\times10^{6}\right)^{3}
     \approx 3.61\times10^{20}\ {\rm m^{3}}
$$

$$
\frac{r^{3}}{\mu}= \frac{3.61\times10^{20}\ {\rm m^{3}}}
{3.986\times10^{14}\ {\rm m^{3}s^{-2}}}
\approx 9.05\times10^{5}\ {\rm s^{2}}
$$

$$
\sqrt{\frac{r^{3}}{\mu}}\approx\sqrt{9.05\times10^{5}}\approx
9.5\times10^{2}\ {\rm s}\approx 951\ {\rm s}
$$

is the time for a radian of motion.  Hence

$$
T =2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^{3}}{\mu}}
   \approx 2\pi\times 9.51\times10^{2}\ {\rm s}
   \approx 5.9\!-\!6.0\times10^{3}\ {\rm s}
$$

$$
T\approx 5.98\times10^{3}\ {\rm s}\approx\frac{5.98\times10^{3}}{60}
      \approx 9.9\!-\!1.0\times10^{1}\ {\rm min}
$$

about $6.0\times10^{3}\ {\rm s}\approx 100\ {\rm min}$ of
mean motion, $\approx 99\!-\!100\;{\rm min}$ for the $7119\;{\rm km}$
geocentric radius, i.e. $\approx 1.66\;{\rm h}$ per revolution.  With
the numbers quoted this is $T\approx 5.98\times10^{3}\ {\rm s}\approx
99.6\ {\rm min}$, essentially the familiar $\sim 100\;{\rm min}$
low-Earth-orbit period.  The $\sim 99.5\;{\rm min}$ value is the
usual textbook $\approx 100\;{\rm min}$ LEO period.

The 9 $\Omega$ is in series with the $R_{2}\parallel R_{3}$ combination

$$R_{2\parallel3}= \frac{R_{2}R_{3}}{R_{2}+R_{3}}
      =\frac{64\times 59}{64+59}
      =\frac{3776}{123}\approx 30.7\;\Omega $$

so that the resistance seen by the ideal 13 V source is

$$R_{T}=R_{1}+R_{2\parallel3}
     =9+\frac{64\times 59}{123}
     =\frac{9\times123+3776}{123}
     =\frac{4883}{123}\approx 39.7\;\Omega $$

The total current taken from the battery, which is the current in the
9 $\Omega$ series resistor, is

$$I_{T}=\frac{V}{R_{T}}=\frac{13}{9+R_{2\parallel3}}
     =\frac{13\times123}{4883}
     =\frac{1599}{4883}\approx 0.33\;{\rm A}$$

in the 9 $\Omega$ and then it divides between $R_{2}$ and $R_{3}$.
The voltage which appears across the parallel pair is

$$V_{p}=V-I_{T}R_{1}=V\frac{R_{2\parallel3}}{R_{1}+R_{2\parallel3}}
      \approx 13\frac{30.70}{39.70}\approx 1.00\times10^{1}\ {\rm V}
      \approx 10.0\!-\!10.1\;{\rm V},$$

the same voltage being across $R_{2}$ and $R_{3}$.  Hence

$$I_{2}=\frac{V_{p}}{R_{2}}
     =\frac{V}{R_{1}+R_{2}R_{3}/(R_{2}+R_{3})}\,
       \frac{R_{3}}{R_{2}+R_{3}}
     =\frac{V\,R_{3}}{R_{1}(R_{2}+R_{3})+R_{2}R_{3}}  $$

i.e. in closed form

$$I_{2}= \frac{13\times 59}{9(64+59)+64\times59}
      =\frac{767}{4883}\ {\rm A}\approx1.57\times10^{-1}\ {\rm A}$$

about $0.16\;{\rm A}$ flowing from the + terminal through $R_{2}$ to
the return.  In the same way

$$I_{3}= \frac{V\,R_{2}}{R_{1}(R_{2}+R_{3})+R_{2}R_{3}}
      =\frac{832}{4883}\approx0.171\;{\rm A},$$

and $I_{2}+I_{3}=I_{T}\approx0.328\;{\rm A}$ as required.  The
$ \approx 10.05\;{\rm V}$ across the $R_{2}\!\parallel\!R_{3}$ pair is
$V_{p}=I_{2}R_{2}=I_{3}R_{3}$, and the drop in the 9 $\Omega$ series
resistor is $I_{T}R_{1}\approx2.95\;{\rm V}$, $2.95+10.05\approx13\;{\rm V}$.

*Remark:* $I_{T}\approx0.33\;{\rm A}$ is the current drawn from the
13 V ideal source, $I_{2}\approx0.157\;{\rm A}\approx157\;{\rm mA}$ is
the part of it that goes through the 64 $\Omega$ resistor, the remainder
$\approx0.171\;{\rm A}$ going through the 59 $\Omega$ resistor.

In the (practically) instantaneous impact the linear momentum in the
horizontal direction is conserved, the kinetic energy is not.  If $V_{0}$ is
the velocity of the $(M+m)$ system immediately after the impact and $v$
the velocity of the bullet just before impact

\begin{equation*}
m\,v=(M+m)V_{0}\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
V_{0}=\frac{m}{M+m}\,v
\end{equation*}

is the velocity with which the pendulum starts to rise.  After the
impact the mechanical energy of the $(M+m)$ pendulum is conserved, up to
the top of the swing,

\begin{equation*}
\frac12\,(M+m)V_{0}^{2}=(M+m)g\,h ,
\end{equation*}

so that the velocity immediately after impact is the velocity which
will raise the centre of mass through the height $h$,

\begin{equation*}
V_{0}=\sqrt{2gh}\;,
\end{equation*}

independently of the masses.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
v=\frac{M+m}{m}V_{0}=\frac{M+m}{m}\sqrt{2gh}
\end{equation*}

is the speed of the bullet in the laboratory frame just before it hits
the block.  With

* $m=28\;{\rm g}=2.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm kg}$,
* $M=3.77\ {\rm kg}$,
* $h=21.9\ {\rm cm}=0.219\ {\rm m}$,

\begin{equation*}
V_{0}=\sqrt{2\times9.81\times0.219}\approx\sqrt{4.30}\approx2.07\ {\rm
m\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the post-impact speed of the block–bullet system.  The corresponding
pre-impact bullet speed is

\begin{equation*}
v=\frac{3.77+0.028}{0.028}\sqrt{2gh}
   \approx 1.36\times10^{2}\times2.07\ {\rm m\,s^{-1}}
   \approx 2.8\!-\!2.9\times10^{2}\ {\rm m\,s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $2.8\times10^{2}\ {\rm m/s}$ in the idealised inelastic
collision.  With the numbers quoted above

\[
V_{0}\simeq2.07\;{\rm m/s},\qquad 
v\simeq\frac{M+m}{m}V_{0}\approx 2.81\times10^{2}\ {\rm m/s}\approx
281\;{\rm m/s}
\]

is obtained.  If the $m\ll M$ correction $M+m\approx M$ is made the
familiar textbook estimate

\[
v\approx\frac{M}{m}\sqrt{2gh}\approx
\frac{3.77}{0.028}\sqrt{2\times9.81\times0.219}\approx2.79\times10^{2}\ {\rm m/s}
\approx 279\!-\!280\;{\rm m/s}
\]

is recovered, essentially the $ \sim 280\;{\rm m/s}$ ballistic-pendulum
value usually quoted.

The series current is the current in $R_{1}$.

$$R_{p}=R_{2}\parallel R_{3}=\frac{R_{2}R_{3}}{R_{2}+R_{3}}
      =\frac{56\times 43}{56+43}\;{\rm \Omega}
      =\frac{2408}{99}\;{\rm \Omega}\approx 24.3\;{\rm \Omega}$$

$$R_{T}=R_{1}+R_{p}=9+\frac{2408}{99}=\frac{3299}{99}\;{\rm \Omega}
      \approx 33.3\;{\rm \Omega}$$

The total current drawn from the ideal $11\;{\rm V}$ source is

$$I_{T}=\frac{V}{R_{T}}=\frac{11}{R_{1}+R_{p}}
     =\frac{11\times 99}{3299}=\frac{1089}{3299}\;{\rm A}
     \approx 0.33\;{\rm A}$$

which is the current in $R_{1}$.  The voltage which appears across the
$R_{2}\parallel R_{3}$ combination is

$$V_{p}=V-I_{T}R_{1}=I_{T}R_{p}
      =\frac{1089}{3299}\frac{2408}{99}\approx 8.0\!-\!8.1\;{\rm V}$$

Hence the branch current in $R_{2}$ is

$$I_{2}=\frac{V_{p}}{R_{2}}=\frac{I_{T}R_{p}}{R_{2}}
     =\frac{V}{R_{1}+R_{p}}\;\frac{R_{3}}{R_{2}+R_{3}}
     =\frac{11}{9+2408/99}\frac{43}{99}\frac1{56}
     =\frac{2\,622\,312}{18\,289\,656}\;{\rm A}
     \approx 1.4\!-\!1.5\times10^{-1}\ {\rm A}$$

i.e. about $0.14\;{\rm A}\approx 143\;{\rm mA}$ flowing from the node
after $R_{1}$ toward the negative terminal of the battery.  In the
usual $R_{p}$ approximation

$V_{p}\approx 11\,{\rm V}\times 24.32/33.32\approx 8.03\,{\rm V}$ and

$I_{2}\approx V_{p}/56\approx 8.03/56\approx 0.14\;{\rm A}$,

the $R_{3}$ branch carrying $I_{3}=V_{p}/43\approx0.19\;{\rm A}$,
$I_{2}+I_{3}=I_{T}\approx0.33\;{\rm A}$, as required.

The induced emf in a coil is Faraday's law

$$\mathcal E = -N\frac{d\Phi}{dt} = -N A\frac{dB}{dt}$$

for a field normal to the plane of the turns.  In magnitude for a
steady change

$$|\mathcal E| = N A\frac{|\Delta B|}{\Delta t}$$

* $N = 372$
* $A = 221\;{\rm cm^{2}} = 221\times10^{-4}\;{\rm m^{2}} = 2.21\times10^{-2}\;{\rm m^{2}}$
* $\Delta B = B_{2}-B_{1}=0.99-0.44 = 0.55\;{\rm T}$
* $\Delta t = 0.21\;{\rm s}$

$$ 
N A = 372\times 0.0221 = 8.22\;{\rm m^{2}} 
$$

$$ 
|\mathcal E| = \frac{N A |\Delta B|}{\Delta t}
           =\frac{8.2212\times0.55}{0.21}
           \approx \frac{4.52}{0.21}\approx 2.15\times10^{1}\ {\rm V}
$$

about $21\!-\!22\;{\rm V}$ emf.  With the coil resistance $R=2.5\;\Omega$

$$ 
I = \frac{|\mathcal E|}{R}
  =\frac{N A |\Delta B|}{R\,\Delta t}
  \approx\frac{21.5\;{\rm V}}{2.5\;\Omega}
  \approx 8.6\;{\rm A}
$$

i.e. $\,I\approx 8\!-\!9\;{\rm A}$ in magnitude, $\approx 8.6\;{\rm A}$ with the numbers quoted, the sign being given by Lenz's law.  This is $\approx 21.5\,{\rm V}/2.5\,\Omega$ induced emf.

The motion is the sum of a uniform motion during the reaction time and a
uniformly decelerated motion after the brakes are applied.

* Speed at the instant the obstacle is seen

$$
v_{0}=50\;{\rm km\,h^{-1}}=\frac{50\times10^{3}}{3600}\;{\rm m\,s^{-1}}
      =\frac{125}{9}\;{\rm m\,s^{-1}}\approx 1.39\times10^{1}\;{\rm m\,s^{-1}}
$$

Reaction distance

During the reaction time $t_{r}$ the car continues at $v_{0}$,

$$
s_{r}=v_{0}t_{r}
$$

$$
s_{r}= \frac{125}{9}\times0.72\;{\rm m}\approx 13.89\times0.72\;{\rm m}
      \approx 1.0\times10^{1}\ {\rm m}
$$

about $10.0\;{\rm m}$ of travel before any braking force is developed.

Braking distance

After the brakes are on $a=5.2\;{\rm m\,s^{-2}}$ is constant and
$v_{f}=0$,

$$
v_{f}^{2}=v_{0}^{2}-2a s_{b}\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
s_{b}=\frac{v_{0}^{2}}{2a}
$$

$$
s_{b}= \frac{\left(13.89\;{\rm m\,s^{-1}}\right)^{2}}{2\times5.2\;{\rm m\,s^{-2}}}
     =\frac{193.0}{10.4}\;{\rm m}\approx 1.86\times10^{1}\ {\rm m}
$$

$$
s_{b}\approx 18.5\!-\!18.6\;{\rm m}
$$

The braking time is $t_{b}=v_{0}/a\approx13.89/5.2\approx2.67\;{\rm s}$.

Total stopping distance from the instant the obstacle is first seen

$$
s_{\rm tot}=s_{r}+s_{b}=v_{0}t_{r}+\frac{v_{0}^{2}}{2a}
$$

$$
s_{\rm tot}\approx 10.0\;{\rm m}+18.5\;{\rm m}\approx 2.8\!-\!2.9\times10^{1}\ {\rm m}
$$

i.e. $\approx 28\!-\!29\;{\rm m}$ from sighting to rest, $\approx 10\;{\rm m}$ of which is travelled during the $0.72\;{\rm s}$ reaction interval and $\approx 18.5\;{\rm m}$ under braking. With the usual $v_{0}=13.89\;{\rm m/s}$ this is $s_{\rm tot}\simeq 28.6\;{\rm m}$, about $28.5\;{\rm m}$ on the exact $125/9\;{\rm m/s}$ value.

The escape speed from a spherical body is

$$v_{e}=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}}$$

so that for two bodies

$$\frac{v_{e,p}}{v_{e,\oplus}}=
\sqrt{\frac{M_{p}}{M_{\oplus}}\frac{R_{\oplus}}{R_{p}}}$$

For the planet

* $M_{p}=2.40\,M_{\oplus}$
* $R_{p}=1.73\,R_{\oplus}$

Hence

$$
v_{e,p}= \sqrt{\frac{2.40}{1.73}}\;
\sqrt{\frac{2GM_{\oplus}}{R_{\oplus}}}
      =\sqrt{1.387}\;v_{e,\oplus}
      \approx 1.18\,v_{e,\oplus}
$$

The terrestrial escape speed is

$$
v_{e,\oplus}= \sqrt{\frac{2GM_{\oplus}}{R_{\oplus}}}
 =\sqrt{\frac{2\times6.674\times10^{-11}\ {\rm SI}\times5.972\times10^{24}\ {\rm kg}}
               {6.371\times10^{6}\ {\rm m}}}
 \approx 1.12\times10^{4}\ {\rm m\,s^{-1}}\approx 11.2\ {\rm km\,s^{-1}}
$$

so that

$$
v_{e,p}\approx 1.18\times 11.2\ {\rm km\,s^{-1}}\approx 1.32\times10^{4}\ {\rm m\,s^{-1}}
$$

i.e. about $13\!-\!13.5\;{\rm km\,s^{-1}}$, $\approx 18\%$ larger than the
Earth value.  With the numbers quoted

$$
v_{e,p}= \sqrt{\frac{2G(2.40M_{\oplus})}{1.73R_{\oplus}}}
       =\sqrt{\frac{2\times6.674\times10^{-11}\times2.40\times5.972\times10^{24}}
                    {1.73\times6.371\times10^{6}}}
       \approx 1.32\times10^{4}\ {\rm m/s}\approx13.2\ {\rm km/s}
$$

which is the usual $ \sqrt{M/R}$ scaling $v_{e}\propto\sqrt{M/R}$ from the
Earth value $11.2\;{\rm km\,s^{-1}}$.  Using $v_{e,\oplus}=11.186\;{\rm km/s}$
gives $v_{e,p}\simeq13.19\;{\rm km/s}$.

For a coil the induced emf is the rate of change of the total flux linkage

$$ {\cal E}= -N\frac{d\Phi}{dt}= -N A\frac{dB}{dt} $$

for a field normal to the plane of the coil and a constant area.

* Area per turn
$$
A =156\;{\rm cm^{2}} =156\times10^{-4}\;{\rm m^{2}}=1.56\times10^{-2}\;{\rm m^{2}}
$$

* Change of flux per turn in the time interval $\Delta t$

$$
\Delta\Phi_{1}=A\,\Delta B
$$

$$
\Delta B = B_{f}-B_{i}=1.20\;{\rm T}-1.40\;{\rm T}=-0.20\;{\rm T}
$$

$$
\Delta\Phi_{1}=0.0156\;{\rm m^{2}}\times(-0.20\;{\rm T})=-3.12\times10^{-3}\;{\rm Wb}
$$

per turn.  For $N$ turns

$$
\Delta\Phi_{N}=N\Delta\Phi_{1}=322\times(-3.12\times10^{-3})\;{\rm Wb}
          =-1.00\;{\rm Wb}
$$

The steady change gives the magnitude of the emf

$$
|{\cal E}|=\left|\frac{\Delta\Phi_{N}}{\Delta t}\right|
        =\frac{N A |\Delta B|}{\Delta t}
$$

$$
|{\cal E}|=
\frac{322\,(0.0156\;{\rm m^{2}})(0.20\;{\rm T})}{0.11\;{\rm s}}
      =\frac{1.00\;{\rm Wb}}{0.11\;{\rm s}}
      \approx 9.1\;{\rm V}
$$

The induced current in the resistance $R$ of the coil is

$$
|I|=\frac{|{\cal E}|}{R}
    =\frac{N A |\Delta B|}{R\,\Delta t}
$$

$$
|I|=\frac{322\times0.0156\times0.20}{17.9\times0.11}\;{\rm A}
    \approx\frac{9.13\;{\rm V}}{17.9\;\Omega}
    \approx 5.1\times10^{-1}\;{\rm A}
$$

about $0.51\;{\rm A}$.  The sign is such that the induced magnetic field tries to oppose the decrease of the applied field, i.e. it acts to keep the flux in the original direction.  The magnitude is $\approx 0.5\!-\!0.52\;{\rm A}$, $\,|{\cal E}|\approx9.1\;{\rm V}$ for the coil.

For a parallel plate

$$C=\frac{\kappa \varepsilon_{0}A}{d}\qquad 
U=\frac12\,C V^{2}=\frac12\,\frac{\kappa\varepsilon_{0}A}{d}V^{2}$$

* Area
$$
A=300\;{\rm cm^{2}}=300\times10^{-4}\;{\rm m^{2}}=3.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm m^{2}}
$$

* Separation
$$
d=1.49\;{\rm mm}=1.49\times10^{-3}\;{\rm m}
$$

* Capacitance with $\kappa=4.0$

$$
\begin{aligned}
C &=\frac{\kappa\varepsilon_{0}A}{d}
   =\frac{4.0\,(8.854\times10^{-12}\ {\rm F/m})\,(3.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm m^{2}})}
          {1.49\times10^{-3}\ {\rm m}}  \\
  &=\frac{1.0625\times10^{-12}}{1.49\times10^{-3}}\ {\rm F}
   \approx 7.1\times10^{-10}\ {\rm F}
\end{aligned}
$$
i.e. $C\approx0.71\ {\rm nF}$.

The electrostatic energy on the $V=98\;{\rm V}$ battery is

$$
\begin{aligned}
U &=\tfrac12 C V^{2}
  =\tfrac12\,(7.13\times10^{-10}\ {\rm F})\,(98\ {\rm V})^{2}\\
  &=\tfrac12\,(7.13\times10^{-10})\,(9.604\times10^{3})\ {\rm J}\\
  &\approx 3.4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm J}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
U\approx 3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm J}\approx 3.4\;\mu{\rm J}
$$

about $3.42\;\mu{\rm J}$ with $\varepsilon_{0}=8.854\times10^{-12}\ {\rm F/m}$,
$U\approx \tfrac12\kappa\varepsilon_{0}A V^{2}/d$.  Using $\varepsilon_{0}\approx8.85\times10^{-12}\ {\rm F/m}$ gives $U\approx3.4\;\mu{\rm J}$, essentially the $ \sim 3.4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm J}$ that a simulation should reproduce.
